  "interpretation": "<p>Matching Ratio Loci Match : 6/12<br>PERCENTAGE:&nbsp; 50.00%</p>\n<p><u><strong>TECHNICAL INFORMATION</strong></u></p>\n<p><strong>PRINCIPLE</strong></p>\n<p>HLA typing by NGS generates high-resolution allelic information in patients and donor candidates in a single step without the need for screening and progression to confirmatory testing. The Test report contains HLA-A, B, C, DRB1, DQB1 and DPB1 alleles.</p>\n<p><strong>ADVANTAGES</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>HLA genes are the most polymorphic gene family found in the human genome, with more than 35,000 different HLA alleles reported in the IMGT database to date.</li>\n<li>High-resolution HLA genotyping by NGS is uniquely able to address the limitations of traditional Sanger sequencing assays in patients requiring a higher level of HLA allele matching.</li>\n<li>HLA typing by NGS covers all the exons and introns for HLA Class I and HLA Class II.</li>\n<li>NGS produces unambiguous results, without the need for NMDP coding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>LIMITATIONS</strong></p>\n<p>All efforts are made to resolve ambiguities. However, in rare cases, this is not possible, and therefore ambiguous alleles for the same loci may be reported. In high resolution, the common null allele will be documented. Null alleles falling in the rare category will not be separately sequenced as assumed as ruled out unless familial data is available which indicates that the rare null allele persists in the family tree.&nbsp;Rare alleles have extremely low frequencies and are not likely to be found repeatedly in a significant number of unrelated subjects. To obtain documentation of rare/null alleles detected in a sample for registry purposes or other clinical purposes, please contact the Department.<br><br></p>\n<div><strong>Note:&nbsp;</strong>For tests performed on specimens received or collected, it is presumed that the specimen belongs to the patient named or identified as labelled on the container/test request and such verification has been carried out at the point generation of the said specimen by the sender. This test has been performed in our outsource partner lab.</div>",
